[General] The opt-in escape hatch only reads the system property, so `OTEL_JAVAAGENT_ENABLED=true` is silently ignored.

Everywhere else `otel.javaagent.enabled` resolves through `EarlyInitAgentConfig.getString()`, which falls back to the env var. `isSecurityManagerSupportEnabled()` a few lines below in this same class does exactly that fallback for its own flag, so the pattern is already established here.

This matters for the deployment referenced above: the OpenTelemetry Operator injects `JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS` *and* the rest of the config as env vars, so a user hitting a false positive would naturally reach for `OTEL_JAVAAGENT_ENABLED=true` and it wouldn't take effect.

```suggestion
    // opt-in is still possible with an explicit otel.javaagent.enabled=true in system properties,
    // java agent arguments or the OTEL_JAVAAGENT_ENABLED environment variable
    Boolean skipJdkTool =
        doPrivileged(
            new PrivilegedAction<Boolean>() {
              @Override
              public Boolean run() {
                String enable = System.getProperty("otel.javaagent.enabled");
                if (enable == null) {
                  enable = System.getenv("OTEL_JAVAAGENT_ENABLED");
                }
                if (Boolean.parseBoolean(enable)) {
                  return false;
                }
                String cmd = System.getProperty("sun.java.command");
                return isJdkToolMainClass(cmd);
```

